## 2. GGUF Split Path Resolution: Symlink Handling

**Component:** `include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp` — `gguf_split_paths()`

**Responsibilities:** Resolves any shard path to the full ordered shard list. Hugging Face snapshot directories use symlinks whose names carry the split suffix but whose targets are hash-named blobs.

**Change:** `std::ifstream(p, std::ios::binary)` follows symlinks on both Windows and Linux, but on Windows a broken symlink (a partially-downloaded snapshot) produces a confusing "cannot open" rather than naming the missing shard. Add a `std::filesystem::exists` check with a clearer message.

```diff
--- a/include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 #pragma once

 #include <cstdio>
+#include <filesystem>
 #include <fstream>
 #include <stdexcept>
 #include <string>
@@ -38,8 +39,9 @@ inline std::vector<std::string> gguf_split_paths(const std::string& any) {
         std::string p = any;
         p.replace(at - 5, 5, num);
-        if (!std::ifstream(p, std::ios::binary))
+        std::error_code ec;
+        if (!std::filesystem::exists(p, ec) || ec)
             throw std::runtime_error("missing model shard " + p + " (shard " + std::to_string(i) + " of " +
-                                     std::to_string(n) + "; is the download complete?)");
+                                     std::to_string(n) + "; is the download complete?");
         out.push_back(std::move(p));
```

**Verification cases:**
1. A complete 4-shard set → returns 4 paths in order.
2. Shard 3 deleted → throws naming the exact missing path and "shard 3 of 4".

